.so man.macros
.TH "policy" n 2.0 Tcl "Safe-Tcl"
.BS
'\" Note:  do not modify the .SH NAME line immediately below!
.SH NAME
policy \-  Tcl command to request use of a security policy.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.sp
\fBpolicy\fR ?\fIpolicyName\fR? ?\fIargs ...\fR?
.BE

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBpolicy\fR command allows a Tclet executing in a safe, untrusted
interpreter to request that a security policy be installed into it, or to
recall the name of the security policy in use if one has been installed.
The \fBpolicy\fR command is available in interpreters created with
\fBsafe::interpCreate\fR or initialized with \fBsafe::interpInit\fR.
.PP
Security policies make unsafe features available in a safe mediated manner
in an untrusted interpreter.
All access to unsafe features is mediated in the untrusted interpreter
through aliases which transfer control to a master interpreter. The master
interpreter can decide whether to allow or deny the access, modify the
arguments and modify the returned result.
An example policy is \fBhome\fR, described in the \fBhome\fR
manual page.
.PP
You should read the section on the \fBPOLICY\fR command to learn how to
invoke the command.
If you are interesting in how the internals of the \fBpolicy\fR command
work, read the section on \fBIMPLEMENTATION OF POLICY COMMAND\fR.
If you want to implement a new security policy, you should read the section
on the \fBIMPLEMENTATION OF SECURITY POLICIES\fR.
If you are a system administrator and want to learn how to install policies
and how to enable or disable use of specific policies, read the sections on
\fBTRUST MAPPING\fR, \fBPOLICY ADMINISTRATION\fR and \fBFILES\fR.
The \fBSECURITY\fR section discusses the general security
considerations that affect your decision to enable or disable a policy.
You should read the \fBconfig\fR manual page to understand configuration
management and the syntax and structure of configuration files.

.SH POLICY
.PP
The \fBpolicy\fR command can be invoked with any number of arguments.
The \fBpolicy\fR command is actually an alias that is executed in the
master interpreter for the invoking interpreter.
.PP
If invoked with no arguments, the \fBpolicy\fR command returns the name of
a policy installed in the invoking interpreter, if one was installed.
If no policy is currently installed, an empty string is returned.
Only one policy may be installed in an interpreter, and it cannot be
uninstalled later.
For example, if a policy named \fBhome\fR was installed in the invoking
interpreter, the \fBpolicy\fR command when invoked with no arguments will
return the string \fBhome\fR:
.PP
.CS
policy
=> home
.CE
.PP
If the \fBpolicy\fR command is invoked with one or more arguments, the
first argument is used as the name of a policy to install, and the rest of
the arguments are passed to the policy initialization procedure (see below).
The command will attempt to install that policy into the invoking interpreter.
For example, to install the \fBgetMyMail\fR policy with several arguments,
the \fBpolicy\fR command could be invoked as follows:
.PP
.CS
policy getMyMail \-name jones \-color green
=> getMyMail
.CE
.PP
If the command succeeds, the policy has been installed in the invoking
interpreter.
The command may also cause an error that can be caught with \fBcatch\fR;
the invoking interpreter can then try to load a different policy.
.PP
The \fBpolicy\fR command can be invoked multiple times with exactly the
same arguments; in that case, after the first invocation, it just returns
the name of the installed policy and doesn't do anything else.
This allows a Tclet to defer obtaining the policy until it is needed and
then request it without having to check whether it was already obtained.

.SH "IMPLEMENTATION OF SECURITY POLICIES"
.PP
Security policies are specifications of features and resources that are
accessible or inaccessible in a Tclet.
As such, they are implemented as configuration files stored in the
directory \fBcfg::configDir\fR.
By convention, the configuration file for a policy is named
\fIpolicy\fB.cfg\fR.
The \fBconfig\fR manual page describes how configuration management works
and the complete syntax of configuration files.
.PP
The \fBpolicy\fR command appears as an atomic action to the invoking
interpreter.
It either succeeds, and then the policy is installed, or it
fails, in which case no side effects are visible in the invoking
interpreter.

.SH "POLICY ADMINISTRATION"
.PP
Edit the configuration file for a policy to administer the resources made
accessible to a Tclet when it obtains that policy.
Several sections are present by convention in each configuration file:
.TP
\fBfeatures\fR
This section enables or disables use of specific named features in a Tclet.
In order to be installed, a feature must be present in the value of
\fBcfg::featuresList\fR; otherwise it will not be installed into a Tclet
even if an \fBallow\fR statement for this feature appears in this section.
See the \fBfeature\fR manual page and manual pages for each feature to
learn what capabilities they provide.
.TP
\fBaliases\fR
This section lists all the aliases that are enabled by this policy.
Individual features check this section to see which of the aliases they
install are allowed by the policy.
If an alias is not allowed in this section, it will not be installed into
the Tclet.
.PP
Other sections may also be present if the policy exercises control over
access to named resources.
For example, the configuration file for the \fBoutside\fR policy,
\fBoutside.cfg\fR, lists the \fBurls\fR and \fBhosts ports\fR section to
control which URLs can be fetched and which ports can be accessed via the
\fBsocket\fR command on what hosts.
.PP
You should exercise care when editing policy configuration files.
Complicated combinations of \fBallow\fR and \fBdisallow\fR statements
should be avoided; they can easily lead to making resources available to a
Tclet in an unintended manner, potentially opening up security holes.

.SH "IMPLEMENTATION OF THE POLICY COMMAND"
.PP
This section explains what the master interpreter does in order to load a
requested policy into a Tclet.
.PP
First, if another policy was already installed in the invoking Tclet,
the command returns an error immediately.
Next, it verifies that the policy is allowed for this Tclet,
as explained in the section on \fBTRUST MAPPING\fR below.
If this check produces an error, it is propagated to the invoking
Tclet. 
.PP
If the security checks succeed, the master interpreter attempts to install
each of the features that is allowed by the requested policy into the Tclet
by invoking the feature installation procedure.
Features are described in the \fBfeature\fR manual page.
If an error occurs at this stage, the interpreter containing the Tclet is
killed to prevent it from executed in an undefined state that may result
from partially installed features.
If no error occurs, the policy is now loaded into the invoking interpreter.

.SH "TRUST MAPPING"
.PP
Policies can be globally enabled or disabled for use across an entire site
by the \fBpolicies\fR section in the application's main configuration file.
The Tcl plugin main configuration file is \fBplugin.cfg\fR in the directory
\fBcfg::configDir\fR.
.PP
During installation of a policy the master interpreter checks whether the
invoking interpreter can use the requested policy.
The master interpreter examines the \fBallow\fR and \fBdisallow\fR
statements in the \fBpolicies\fR section of \fBplugin.cfg\fR.
If the policy is disallowed the requesting Tclet can not use that policy
and the \fBpolicy\fR command will return an error.
If the policy is not mentioned in this section, no Tclet can use the
policy.
.PP
To install a new policy and enable it, you should edit the \fBplugin.cfg\fR
file and add an \fBallow\fR statement that enables the policy in exactly
the intended circumstances.
To disable a policy completely, ensure that there are no \fBallow\fR
statements in this section for the policy.
If you want to disallow the policy under some condition (e.g. when the
Tclet is loaded from a certain host), add a \fBdisallow\fR statement that
explicitly tests for that condition.

.SH SECURITY
.PP
You should understand exactly what resources and capabilities each
policy makes accessible to Tclets.
Tclets that come from untrusted sources should generally not be given
access to sensitive resources.
For example, because the \fBinside\fR policy is intended to allow
access to resources on your company's Intranet, the default
configuration file for the Tcl plugin disables this policy.
.PP
The risks incurred by policies stem from the combination of features
each policy enables.
These risks are listed in the manual entries for each feature, and the
manual entry for each policy lists what features it enables; this
allows you to quickly oversee the risks incurred by a specific policy.
.PP
The security risks can be divided into four general classes:
.TP
\fBIntegrity risks\fR
A feature may include a capability that lets a malicious Tclet destroy
a sensitive resource on the client's host.
For example, the feature might enable a Tclet to remove files on the
local disk, and a malicious Tclet might use it to destroy all files.
The Safe Base is designed to protect the integrity of a computer on
which Tclets are executed.
Policies may relax restrictions imposed for this purpose on Tclets,
but relaxing these restrictions also weakens the protection.
.TP
\fBPrivacy risks\fR
A feature may enable a malicious Tclet to obtain sensitive information
and leak it to other parties.
Even one bit of information when leaked in this way may be very
damaging; for example, whether the owner of the computer on which the
Tclet is being executed has even been arrested.
The Safe Base is designed to prevent privacy attacks.
However, by relaxing restrictions imposed on a Tclet for this purpose,
policies may expose the hosting computer or its owner to some privacy
attacks.
.TP
\fBRisk of annoyance and resource attacks\fR
A feature may allow a malicious Tclet to perform operations that
disrupt the work of the human using the hosting computer, such as
ringing the bell or opening a large number of windows on the
computer's display.
Or, the Tclet could try to exhaust some finite resource on the hosting
computer, such as storage or memory space.
At present, the Safe Base is not attempting to prevent all annoyance
and resource attacks.
.TP
\fBLoss of reputation\fR
A Tclet may gain access to a resource or capability that can be used
to perform actions that are detrimental to the reputation of the human
owner of the hosting computer.
For example, a malicious Tclet could send bogus or inflamatory
electronic mail, or post netnews articles, making it appear that
the user of the computer is the one who actually sent the email or
posted the articles.
While the Safe Base prevents such attacks, individual policies may
expose the user of the computer on which Tclets are executing to these
risks by relaxing some of the attendant restrictions.
.PP
The policies provided with the Tcl plugin make a variety of resources
available to Tclets.
The policies have been carefully designed and documented to minimze
the risks associated with their use.
However, some policies are disabled by default because they provide
access to features that would be dangerous in the hands of potentially
malicious Tclets.
.PP
The 2.0 release of the Tcl plugin provides no mechanism for
authenticating the author of a Tclet or web page, and thus has no way
to associate a higher level of trust with a Tclet.
In controlled situations such as when the plugin is used to view
Tclets loaded from servers on a company's Intranet, it may be
sufficient to trust the URL from which a Tclet is loaded.
However, this is a very weak form of authentication and can be
subverted when used for authenticating Tclets loaded from sites not
under your control.
.PP
All of the above means that you carefully control, through settings in
the application's master configuration, which Tclets can use
each policy.
Incorrect configuration exposes your computer and potentially other
computers on the same network to the risks discussed above.

.SH FILES
.PP
Policy configuration files are stored, along with other configuration
files, in the \fBcfg::configDir\fR directory.
Policy configuration files are named \fIpolicy\fB.cfg\fR.
.PP
The use of policies in an application is controlled by settings in the
\fBpolicies\fR section in the master configuration for that
application.
For the Tcl plugin, the master configuration is contained in the
\fBplugin.cfg\fR file in the \fBcfg::configDir\fR directory.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
safe(n), config(n), feature(n)

.SH KEYWORDS
Safe Base, policies, trust predicates
